[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The issue is not a router1 uplink failure. The affected component is routing on router0 (FRR/RIP), which is missing return-path routes to the Office and Principle Room subnets. Mechanism: Office/Principle hosts (192.168.3.0/24, 192.168.4.0/24) send traffic to CS/IT (192.168.2.0/24, 192.168.1.0/24) via router1. Router1 forwards correctly over its WAN uplink eth2 (10.10.0.2) to router0 (10.10.0.1); link is healthy (eth2 link detected: yes), and router1 can ping 10.10.0.1, 192.168.1.1/1.2, 192.168.2.1/2.2 successfully. However, router0 lacks routes back to 192.168.3.0/24 and 192.168.4.0/24 (its ip route shows only 10.0.0.0/8, 192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.2.0/24), and FRR on router0 has no RIP configuration, so it does not learn or advertise those networks. As a result, replies from CS/IT die at router0 (no route to 192.168.3/4), producing the observed timeouts from Office/Principle while tests sourced from router1 succeed.

Localization: router0, interface eth2 (10.10.0.1) path towards router1; the fault is routing control-plane, not a physical/link fault. Root cause: FRR on router0 is not configured to learn/advertise routes to 192.168.3.0/24 and 192.168.4.0/24, leaving no return path for those subnets.

Corrective fix: Enable RIP on router0 (router rip; version 2; network 10.0.0.0/8; network 192.168.1.0/24; network 192.168.2.0/24) or add static routes to 192.168.3.0/24 and 192.168.4.0/24 via 10.10.0.2. After applying, restart FRR and verify reachability. Router1’s uplink eth2 is operating correctly. 
=== END ===